1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,632
Welcome to chapter eleven of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,632 --> 00:00:18,444
Today we assemble everything into a complete GPT: a config object, a stack of transformer blocks, a final layer norm, a language modelling head and weight tying.

3
00:00:18,444 --> 00:00:24,387
Then we run five sanity checks and load the real GPT-2 weights into our own code.

4
00:00:24,387 --> 00:00:41,481
And that's where my run of the notebook turned up the most interesting bug in the series so far: the loaded model produces garbage, and I'll show you exactly why, and the two line fix that makes it match GPT-2 to four decimal places.

5
00:00:42,331 --> 00:00:47,962
We built embeddings in chapter nine and the transformer block in chapter ten.

6
00:00:47,962 --> 00:00:54,105
Today we stack the blocks into a complete model and load real GPT-2 weights into it.

7
00:00:54,105 --> 00:00:56,664
Next chapter we train it ourselves.

8
00:00:56,664 --> 00:01:00,101
Everything follows the chapter eleven notebook.

9
00:01:00,951 --> 00:01:02,175
Here's the plan.

10
00:01:02,175 --> 00:01:04,546
What a single block is missing.

11
00:01:04,546 --> 00:01:06,534
The configuration pattern.

12
00:01:06,534 --> 00:01:08,140
The full model class.

13
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:09,135
Weight tying.

14
00:01:09,135 --> 00:01:10,588
Five sanity checks.

15
00:01:10,588 --> 00:01:13,264
A forward pass with random weights.

16
00:01:13,264 --> 00:01:16,706
Loading GPT-2, including the bug and the fix.

17
00:01:16,706 --> 00:01:21,218
Then training versus evaluation mode, exercises and review.

18
00:01:22,068 --> 00:01:22,908
A few terms.

19
00:01:22,908 --> 00:01:30,672
The language modelling head maps a seven sixty eight hidden state to a score for each of fifty thousand tokens.

20
00:01:30,672 --> 00:01:32,631
Those raw scores are logits.

21
00:01:32,631 --> 00:01:36,479
Weight tying reuses the embedding matrix for that head.

22
00:01:36,479 --> 00:01:39,836
Module list registers a list of layers properly.

23
00:01:39,836 --> 00:01:42,844
A config object holds every hyperparameter.

24
00:01:42,844 --> 00:01:46,132
And greedy decoding always picks the top token.

25
00:01:46,982 --> 00:01:47,580
Part one.

26
00:01:47,580 --> 00:01:49,969
From a single block to a full model.

27
00:01:50,819 --> 00:01:52,423
Here's the whole model.

28
00:01:52,423 --> 00:01:59,328
Token IDs go into the token and position embeddings, giving batch by sequence by seven sixty eight.

29
00:01:59,328 --> 00:02:07,907
Then twelve identical blocks, each with layer norm, attention and a residual, then layer norm, feed forward and a residual.

30
00:02:07,907 --> 00:02:11,046
The shape stays the same all the way through.

31
00:02:11,046 --> 00:02:18,927
A final layer norm, and then the head projects to fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven scores per position.

32
00:02:18,927 --> 00:02:23,879
The dashed arrow is weight tying: the head reuses the embedding matrix.

33
00:02:24,730 --> 00:02:26,491
Why stack identical blocks?

34
00:02:26,491 --> 00:02:34,384
Every block has the same architecture but its own learned weights, and each one adds a refinement to the residual stream.

35
00:02:34,384 --> 00:02:45,017
Research that probes trained models finds a rough trend: earlier layers capture more local and syntactic patterns, later ones more semantic and task specific ones.

36
00:02:45,017 --> 00:02:48,214
The book uses a team of detectives as an analogy.

37
00:02:48,214 --> 00:02:52,911
It's a helpful picture, but the real division of labour is much messier.

38
00:02:53,761 --> 00:02:54,438
Part two.

39
00:02:54,438 --> 00:02:56,321
The configuration object.

40
00:02:57,171 --> 00:03:02,743
All seven hyperparameters live in one dataclass, with GPT-2 small as the default.

41
00:03:02,743 --> 00:03:10,860
Post init runs right after construction and checks that the embedding dimension divides evenly by the number of heads.

42
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:15,400
A tiny model for experiments is just a different set of arguments.

43
00:03:15,400 --> 00:03:23,518
And because it's a dataclass, a typo in a field name is an error, not a silently created new key as with a dictionary.

44
00:03:24,367 --> 00:03:25,355
Part three.

45
00:03:25,355 --> 00:03:26,970
The MiniGPT class.

46
00:03:27,820 --> 00:03:41,567
The constructor has the two embedding tables and dropout, a module list of transformer blocks, the final layer norm, and the head, a linear layer from seven sixty eight to the vocabulary with no bias.

47
00:03:41,567 --> 00:03:48,577
The last line ties the weights: the head's weight becomes the very same tensor as the token embedding.

48
00:03:49,427 --> 00:03:50,614
Why a module list?

49
00:03:50,614 --> 00:03:53,909
A plain Python list hides the blocks from PyTorch.

50
00:03:53,909 --> 00:04:01,752
They don't appear in model dot parameters, they don't move with dot to device, and they aren't saved in the state dict.

51
00:04:01,752 --> 00:04:08,343
The optimizer would never update them, so training would appear to run while the blocks stay random.

52
00:04:08,343 --> 00:04:10,057
It's a classic silent bug.

53
00:04:10,907 --> 00:04:12,101
The forward pass.

54
00:04:12,101 --> 00:04:18,565
Embed tokens and positions and add them; the position embeddings broadcast across the batch.

55
00:04:18,565 --> 00:04:21,165
Build a lower triangular causal mask.

56
00:04:21,165 --> 00:04:24,537
Run every block, final layer norm, and the head.

57
00:04:24,537 --> 00:04:34,373
The output is batch by sequence by vocabulary: a full set of next token scores at every position, which is what makes training so efficient.

58
00:04:35,223 --> 00:04:39,322
Logits are a raw score for every token at every position.

59
00:04:39,322 --> 00:04:42,054
Softmax turns them into probabilities.

60
00:04:42,054 --> 00:04:50,250
During training, every position predicts its own next token, so one sequence gives many training examples at once.

61
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:54,061
During generation, we only look at the last position.

62
00:04:54,911 --> 00:04:55,699
Part four.

63
00:04:55,699 --> 00:04:56,724
Weight tying.

64
00:04:57,574 --> 00:05:00,594
The embedding matrix maps an ID to a vector.

65
00:05:00,594 --> 00:05:07,251
The head maps a vector to scores by multiplying with the transpose of a matrix of the same shape.

66
00:05:07,251 --> 00:05:14,526
Tie them, and you save thirty eight point six million parameters, about thirty one percent of GPT-2 small.

67
00:05:14,526 --> 00:05:23,174
In PyTorch, assigning the weight attribute makes both modules point at one tensor; an update through either path changes both.

68
00:05:24,024 --> 00:05:25,852
The book lists four gotchas.

69
00:05:25,852 --> 00:05:26,896
Three are right.

70
00:05:26,896 --> 00:05:31,530
But it says lm head dot weight shouldn't appear in the state dict keys.

71
00:05:31,530 --> 00:05:34,859
In my run, it does: the state dict lists both keys.

72
00:05:34,859 --> 00:05:41,386
They share storage, so torch dot save writes the data only once and the file doesn't double in size.

73
00:05:41,386 --> 00:05:50,328
Also remember to tie only after both layers exist, and if you ever replace the tensor rather than copying into it, you have to tie again.

74
00:05:51,178 --> 00:05:51,901
Part five.

75
00:05:51,901 --> 00:05:54,720
Five sanity checks before any training.

76
00:05:55,569 --> 00:05:58,083
The five checks all passed on my CPU.

77
00:05:58,083 --> 00:06:04,945
Shape: two by sixteen IDs become two by sixteen by fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven logits.

78
00:06:04,945 --> 00:06:06,032
Parameter count.

79
00:06:06,032 --> 00:06:12,417
Causal masking: changing only the last token leaves the logits at earlier positions identical.

80
00:06:12,417 --> 00:06:16,494
Weight tying: the two weights are literally the same object.

81
00:06:16,494 --> 00:06:20,230
And gradient flow: every parameter receives a gradient.

82
00:06:20,230 --> 00:06:23,559
Keep an eye on that parameter count; it's a clue.

83
00:06:24,409 --> 00:06:26,544
The causal test is my favourite.

84
00:06:26,544 --> 00:06:29,613
Two inputs that differ only in the last token.

85
00:06:29,613 --> 00:06:37,553
Positions zero to two must produce identical logits, because they can't see the future, and position three must differ.

86
00:06:37,553 --> 00:06:46,894
If the mask were broken, the model could peek at the answer during training: the loss would look fantastic, and generation would be useless.

87
00:06:47,744 --> 00:07:00,692
Here's where GPT-2 small's parameters live, from my own count, which matches Hugging Face exactly at one hundred and twenty four million four hundred and thirty nine thousand eight hundred and eight.

88
00:07:00,692 --> 00:07:05,117
The feed forward layers are the biggest share at forty five percent.

89
00:07:05,117 --> 00:07:12,469
The tied embedding is thirty one percent, attention twenty three percent, and positions and layer norms are tiny.

90
00:07:13,319 --> 00:07:14,599
And here's the clue.

91
00:07:14,599 --> 00:07:23,366
The book prints one hundred and twenty four million four hundred and thirty nine thousand eight hundred and eight, the real GPT-2 number.

92
00:07:23,366 --> 00:07:29,445
But the notebook's model has thirty six thousand eight hundred and sixty four fewer parameters.

93
00:07:29,445 --> 00:07:39,876
That's exactly twelve layers times two thousand three hundred and four plus seven hundred and sixty eight: the query key value bias and the output projection bias.

94
00:07:39,876 --> 00:07:45,059
The notebook's attention is built without biases, but GPT-2's attention has them.

95
00:07:45,059 --> 00:07:46,211
Keep that in mind.

96
00:07:46,211 --> 00:07:57,858
As a small aside, the book's rough formula evaluates to one hundred and twenty four million three hundred and fifty six thousand eight hundred and sixty four, not the number printed.

97
00:07:58,707 --> 00:07:59,327
Part six.

98
00:07:59,327 --> 00:08:02,355
Our first forward pass, with random weights.

99
00:08:03,205 --> 00:08:17,045
With random weights, the four token prompt gives a one by four by fifty thousand logits tensor, and the top predictions are random junk like RC and Abel, each with probability about one in ten thousand.

100
00:08:17,045 --> 00:08:21,567
An untrained model is close to uniform over fifty thousand tokens.

101
00:08:21,567 --> 00:08:31,090
That gives a useful number for chapter twelve: the starting loss should be about the natural log of fifty thousand, around ten point eight.

102
00:08:31,090 --> 00:08:34,858
If your first loss is far from that, something's wrong.

103
00:08:35,708 --> 00:08:36,556
Part seven.

104
00:08:36,556 --> 00:08:40,103
Loading GPT-2's weights, the bug, and the fix.

105
00:08:40,953 --> 00:08:52,117
To load GPT-2, we map names: w t e to token embed, h dot i dot l n one to our layer norms, c attn to our combined query key value projection, and so on.

106
00:08:52,117 --> 00:09:04,163
The projection weights must be transposed, because GPT-2's original code uses a Conv1D layer that stores weights as in by out, while nn dot Linear stores out by in.

107
00:09:04,163 --> 00:09:09,966
Notice what's missing from this table: there's no row for the attention biases.

108
00:09:10,816 --> 00:09:14,948
Here's what the notebook actually generated after loading GPT-2.

109
00:09:14,948 --> 00:09:16,755
The quick brown foxeseseses.

110
00:09:16,755 --> 00:09:19,918
Artificial intelligence will be used to to to to.

111
00:09:19,918 --> 00:09:22,565
The capital of France is France is is is.

112
00:09:22,565 --> 00:09:28,310
The book shows a fluent continuation about the lazy dog, but this code cannot produce it.

113
00:09:28,310 --> 00:09:34,830
And notice: the weights loaded without errors, the shapes all matched, and every sanity check passed.

114
00:09:34,830 --> 00:09:36,508
The model is still broken.

115
00:09:36,508 --> 00:09:38,187
That's a lesson in itself.

116
00:09:39,037 --> 00:09:45,320
To debug, I compared our model's logits with Hugging Face's GPT-2 on the same input.

117
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:51,603
The largest difference was fifty eight, which is not rounding; something is missing.

118
00:09:51,603 --> 00:09:59,606
Step two: I added the attention biases that the loader was silently dropping, and copied in GPT-2's values.

119
00:09:59,606 --> 00:10:02,224
The error fell to seven hundredths.

120
00:10:02,224 --> 00:10:09,480
Step three: GPT-2 uses the tanh approximation of GELU, while the notebook uses the exact version.

121
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:14,341
Switching that brought the error down to six hundred thousandths.

122
00:10:14,341 --> 00:10:18,156
Now our model computes exactly what GPT-2 computes.

123
00:10:19,006 --> 00:10:22,417
Here's the patch, my own code on top of the notebook.

124
00:10:22,417 --> 00:10:25,312
Keep the biases in the attention projections.

125
00:10:25,312 --> 00:10:26,856
Copy them in the loader.

126
00:10:26,856 --> 00:10:28,272
And use the tanh GELU.

127
00:10:28,272 --> 00:10:38,825
With that, the parameter count becomes one hundred and twenty four million four hundred and thirty nine thousand eight hundred and eight, identical to Hugging Face.

128
00:10:39,675 --> 00:10:43,514
After the fix, the same model with the same weights is fluent.

129
00:10:43,514 --> 00:10:47,725
Foxes are a great way to get a little bit of a kick out of your dog.

130
00:10:47,725 --> 00:10:52,245
Once upon a time, the world was a place of great beauty and great danger.

131
00:10:52,245 --> 00:11:00,481
It still repeats itself, but that's greedy decoding, which always picks the top token, not a bug; sampling comes in chapter fourteen.

132
00:11:00,481 --> 00:11:07,664
On my CPU, thirty tokens took about ten seconds, because this simple loop recomputes the whole prefix at every step.

133
00:11:08,514 --> 00:11:10,856
The lesson is bigger than this bug.

134
00:11:10,856 --> 00:11:14,938
All five sanity checks passed, and the model was still wrong.

135
00:11:14,938 --> 00:11:17,748
Those checks test wiring, not correctness.

136
00:11:17,748 --> 00:11:24,507
When a reference implementation exists, compare the actual numbers, as a maximum absolute difference.

137
00:11:24,507 --> 00:11:32,001
Typical silent mismatches are missing biases, the GELU variant, layer norm epsilon, transposes and tied weights.

138
00:11:32,001 --> 00:11:34,477
I'd add this as a sixth sanity check.

139
00:11:35,327 --> 00:11:37,083
A few practical details.

140
00:11:37,083 --> 00:11:43,084
Train mode enables dropout, eval mode disables it, so call eval before generating.

141
00:11:43,084 --> 00:11:47,694
Wrap generation in no grad to skip building the autograd graph.

142
00:11:47,694 --> 00:11:51,646
Move both the model and the inputs to the same device.

143
00:11:51,646 --> 00:11:59,476
And a preview of chapter twelve: weight decay is usually not applied to biases, layer norms and embeddings.

144
00:12:00,326 --> 00:12:01,140
Part eight.

145
00:12:01,140 --> 00:12:02,694
Exercises and review.

146
00:12:03,544 --> 00:12:04,765
Exercise answers.

147
00:12:04,765 --> 00:12:22,360
The tiny checkpoint model has one hundred and thirty two thousand six hundred and eight parameters, not about one hundred and fifty thousand as the book says, or one hundred and thirty three thousand one hundred and twenty with attention biases.

148
00:12:22,360 --> 00:12:27,889
The full breakdown for GPT-2 small is on screen, with zero for the tied head.

149
00:12:27,889 --> 00:12:30,978
The causal mask is always a lower triangle.

150
00:12:30,978 --> 00:12:37,656
With the fixed model, def fibonacci produces return n in a loop: GPT-2 small is weak at code.

151
00:12:37,656 --> 00:12:42,109
And temperature only matters if you sample; argmax ignores it.

152
00:12:42,959 --> 00:12:44,056
Key takeaways.

153
00:12:44,056 --> 00:12:50,323
A GPT is embeddings, a stack of identical blocks, a final layer norm and a head.

154
00:12:50,323 --> 00:12:56,982
A config dataclass keeps experiments clean, and module list keeps parameters visible.

155
00:12:56,982 --> 00:13:00,742
Weight tying saves about a third of GPT-2 small.

156
00:13:00,742 --> 00:13:06,383
Sanity checks test wiring; comparing with a reference tests correctness.

157
00:13:06,383 --> 00:13:12,258
Loading GPT-2 needs the transposes, the attention biases and the tanh GELU.

158
00:13:12,258 --> 00:13:16,724
And a random model starts near a loss of ten point eight.

159
00:13:17,574 --> 00:13:20,144
The notebook ran completely on my CPU.

160
00:13:20,144 --> 00:13:40,637
The book checks: the loaded GPT-2 degenerates because of the missing attention biases and the GELU variant, which we fixed; the parameter count differs for the same reason; the lm head key does appear in the state dict; and the checkpoint model is about one hundred and thirty three thousand parameters.

161
00:13:40,637 --> 00:13:42,192
All in the errata file.

162
00:13:43,042 --> 00:13:44,498
Pause and try these.

163
00:13:44,498 --> 00:13:45,662
The four pieces.

164
00:13:45,662 --> 00:13:46,827
Why a dataclass.

165
00:13:46,827 --> 00:13:48,937
The output shape of the head.

166
00:13:48,937 --> 00:13:50,611
The savings from tying.

167
00:13:50,611 --> 00:13:54,905
Why earlier positions must be unchanged in the causal test.

168
00:13:54,905 --> 00:13:56,142
Why we transpose.

169
00:13:56,142 --> 00:14:01,965
And the big one: why could every sanity check pass while generation was garbage?

170
00:14:02,815 --> 00:14:06,985
To practise: run the notebook and reproduce the broken output.

171
00:14:06,985 --> 00:14:13,239
Apply the three line fix and verify the difference with Hugging Face is under one thousandth.

172
00:14:13,239 --> 00:14:15,324
Count parameters per component.

173
00:14:15,324 --> 00:14:18,687
And swap greedy decoding for temperature sampling.

174
00:14:19,537 --> 00:14:22,280
Next time, chapter twelve: training.

175
00:14:22,280 --> 00:14:31,654
The full loop, cross entropy, warmup, gradient clipping and perplexity, training a small MiniGPT on Shakespeare, on my CPU.

